WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.789
In computer games and all kinds of applications
where we are yearning for realistic animation,

00:00:09.789 --> 00:00:14.839
we somehow need to tell our computers how
the different joints and body parts of these

00:00:14.839 --> 00:00:18.630
virtual characters are meant to move around
over time.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:24.210
Since the human eye is very sensitive to even
the tiniest inaccuracies, we typically don’t

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:29.311
program these motions by hand, but instead,
we often record a lot of real-life motion

00:00:29.311 --> 00:00:34.309
capture data in a studio, and try to reuse
that in our applications.

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:39.589
Previous techniques have tackled quadruped
control, and we can even teach bipeds to interact

00:00:39.589 --> 00:00:42.339
with their environment in a realistic manner.

00:00:42.339 --> 00:00:46.949
Today we will have a look at an absolutely
magnificent piece of work, where the authors

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:52.449
carved out a smaller subproblem, and made
a solution for it that is truly second to

00:00:52.449 --> 00:00:53.449
none.

00:00:53.449 --> 00:00:58.370
And this subproblem is simulating virtual
characters playing basketball.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:02.710
Like with previous works, we are looking for
realism in the movement, and for games, it

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:07.040
is also a requirement that the character responds
to our controls well.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:13.080
However, the key challenge is that all we
are given, is 3 hours of unstructured motion

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.600
capture data.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:20.140
That is next to nothing, and from this next
to nothing, a learning algorithm has to learn

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:25.610
to understand these motions so well, that
it can weave them together, even when a specific

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.050
movement combination is not present in this
data.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.080
That is quite a challenge.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:36.260
Compared to many other works, this data is
really not a lot, so I am excited to see what

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.220
value we are getting out of these three hours.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:45.760
At first I thought we’d get only very rudimentary
motions, and boy, was I dead wrong on that

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:46.760
one.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.960
We have control over this character and can
perform these elaborate maneuvers, and it

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.550
remains very responsive even if we mash the
controller like a madman, producing these

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.180
sharp turns.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.580
As you see, it can handle these cases really
well.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:06.990
And not only that, but it is so well done,
we can even dribble through a set of obstacles,

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.250
leading to a responsive, and enjoyable gameplay.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.610
About these dribbling behaviors.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.840
Do we get only one, boring motion, or not?

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:22.160
Not at all, it was able to mine out not just
one, but many kinds of dribbling motions,

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:27.870
and is able to weave them into other moves
as soon as we interact with the controller.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:33.700
This is already very convincing, especially
from just three hours of unstructured motion

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:34.700
capture data.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.850
But this paper is just getting started.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:43.630
Now, hold on to your papers, because we can
also shoot and catch the ball, move it around,

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:50.170
that is very surprising, because it has looked
at so little shooting data, let’s see…yes,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.430
less than 7 minutes.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:53.950
My goodness.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:59.390
And it keeps going, what I have found even
more surprising is that it can handle unexpected

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:04.690
movements, which I find to be even more remarkable
given the limited training data.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:09.650
These crazy corner cases are typically learnable
when they are available in abundance in the

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:12.950
training data, which is not the case here.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:14.040
Amazing.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:18.910
When we compare these motions to a previous
method, we see that both the character and

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.680
the ball’s movement is much more lively.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.440
For instance, here, you can see that the Phase-Functioned
Neural Network, PFNN in short, almost makes

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.819
it seem like the ball has to stick to the
hand of the player for an unhealthy amount

00:03:31.819 --> 00:03:35.130
of time to be able to create these motions.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.510
It doesn’t happen at all with the new technique.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:43.690
And remember, this new method is also much
more responsive to the player’s controls,

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:48.180
and thus, more enjoyable not only to look
at, but to play with.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.770
This is an aspect that hard is hard to measure,
but it is not to be underestimated in the

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.989
general playing experience.

00:03:54.989 --> 00:04:00.430
Just imagine what this research area will
be capable of not in a decade, but just two

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.160
more papers down the line.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.360
Loving it.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.100
Now, at the start of the video, I noted that
the authors carved out a small use-case, which

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:15.800
is training an AI to weave together basketball
motion capture data in a manner that is both

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.430
realistic and controllable.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:24.120
However, many times in research, we look at
a specialized problem, and during that journey

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:29.130
we learn general concepts that can be applied
to other problems as well.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:34.250
That is exactly what happened here, as you
see, parts of this technique can be generalized

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:36.490
for quadruped control as well.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:40.680
This good boy is pacing and running around
beautifully.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:46.500
And…you guessed right, our favorite biped
from the previous paper is also making an

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:47.660
introduction.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:52.620
I am absolutely spellbound by this work, and
I hope that now, you are too.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:57.850
Can’t wait to see this implemented in newer
games and other real-time applications.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:53.130
What a time to be alive!

00:05:53.130 --> 00:06:01.300
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:02.789
and I'll see you next time!

